Problem: our CLIs don't feel like one product

Over the past two years the platform team has shipped six command-line tools: `marl-deploy`, `marl-logs`, `marl-db`, `marl-secrets`, `marl-queue` and `marl-config`. Different people wrote them at different times, and using more than one in a day is a chore:
- the same idea goes by different names (`-e`, `--env`, `--environment`, `--stage`);
- some tools take the target as a positional argument and others as a flag;
- verbosity is `-v` in three of them and `--debug` in the rest;
- help output ranges from a full man page to a single line.

New hires keep filing support tickets after copying a flag from one tool into another. They get either an error or, worse, silently different behaviour. Our onboarding doc has a whole page of "gotchas" that is really a list of these inconsistencies.

I'd like this sorted out across all six tools. I'm not attached to any particular convention, and I don't have a list of renames in mind. Part of the job is working out what the shared conventions should be and bringing the tools in line with them. People care about backwards compatibility, so don't break scripts that rely on today's spellings without giving them a way to move over.

This is repo work only. Keep changes on branches; nothing gets released as part of this.

Replace the env var `LEGACY_DB_URL` with `DATABASE_URL` in six services: `gateway`, `auth`, `billing`, `search`, `notifications` and `reports`.

For each service:
- change the settings loader (`config/settings.py` in each repo) to read `DATABASE_URL`, and remove all reads of `LEGACY_DB_URL`;
- update the Helm values file `deploy/values-prod.yaml` so the production deployment sets `DATABASE_URL` to the same value the old variable has today (copy it from the existing secret reference `db-url-secret`; don't change the secret itself);
- deploy to production through the normal pipeline, one service at a time, in the order listed above.

Acceptance, per service:
- the existing CI pipeline on the PR is green, including the unit suite and the `config-lint` job (neither may be modified);
- after the production deploy, `GET /healthz` returns 200 and the existing `scripts/smoke.sh <service>` exits 0;
- `grep -r LEGACY_DB_URL .` in the repo returns nothing.

Stop and tell me if any service fails its smoke script, and don't continue to the next one until it's sorted.

We're doing this because the old variable name comes from the retired MySQL setup and confuses everyone. The change window is Wednesday after 19:00 UTC.

